<h1>Ed25519 Signing</h1>
<p class="subtitle">Local change-detection evidence with no automatic refusal gate.</p>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Current load tools do not enforce integrity.</strong> <code>load_skill</code>, <code>load_section</code>, and <code>find_and_load</code> can return content without refusing <code>TAMPERED</code> or attaching a current status. A caller must enforce its own policy.</div>

<h2 id="files">Keys and manifest</h2>
<p>The installer creates:</p>
<div class="code-block"><span class="dim">~/.mcp-librarian/</span>
<span class="dim">  keys/private.pem</span>   <span class="cmt"># PKCS8 Ed25519 private key</span>
<span class="dim">  keys/public.pem</span>    <span class="cmt"># SPKI Ed25519 public key</span>
<span class="dim">  manifest.json</span>      <span class="cmt"># one entry per signed skill name</span></div>
<p>Both PEM files are written with mode <code>0600</code>. For each flat skill, the signer stores a SHA-256 content hash, an Ed25519 signature over <code>hash|signedAt</code>, and the timestamp. The manifest also has a top-level timestamp.</p>

<h2 id="when-signing-runs">When signing runs</h2>
<ul>
  <li><code>node bin/mcp-librarian.js sign</code> signs every top-level <code>.md</code> file using <code>keys/private.pem</code>.</li>
  <li>A successful <code>create_skill</code> attempts the same all-skills signing pass before rebuilding.</li>
  <li><code>install_pack</code> attempts it after at least one install/update, before rebuilding.</li>
  <li>If the private key is absent, runtime signing is a silent no-op. <code>create_skill</code> reports <code>signed: false</code>; <code>install_pack</code> does not expose a signing flag.</li>
</ul>
<p>The fresh installer copies <code>getting-started.md</code> after creating a skeleton manifest but does not run the signer, so the starter normally begins as <code>UNSIGNED</code>.</p>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Signing is library-wide.</strong> A supported write re-signs all flat skills, not only the new file. Review direct disk changes first, or the signing pass can incorporate them into a fresh manifest.</div>

<h2 id="verification">When verification runs</h2>
<p>The store reads <code>manifest.json</code> and a public PEM while loading or rebuilding. It calculates one status per indexed skill:</p>
<table class="cmd-table"><thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning at that load/rebuild</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>VERIFIED</code></td><td>The file content matched the manifest hash and its signature verified under the loaded public key.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>TAMPERED</code></td><td>The hash differed, the signature failed, or signature verification threw.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>UNSIGNED</code></td><td>No usable manifest entry and public key combination was available for that skill.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

<p>There is no filesystem watcher. <code>skill_status</code> returns the stored snapshot; it does not reread or rehash the file. Only a restart or supported mutation rebuilds that status. Skill content is also initially placed in an LRU cache, so a disk edit may continue returning cached old content until eviction or expiry; a later disk read still does not refresh integrity status.</p>

<h2 id="inspect">Inspect status</h2>
<div class="code-block">skill_status({ <span class="kw">skill</span>: <span class="str">"api-design"</span> })

{
  <span class="kw">"name"</span>: <span class="str">"api-design"</span>,
  <span class="kw">"integrity"</span>: <span class="str">"VERIFIED"</span>,
  <span class="kw">"hash"</span>: <span class="str">"64-character SHA-256 hex"</span>,
  <span class="kw">"signedAt"</span>: <span class="str">"2026-...Z"</span>,
  <span class="kw">"quality"</span>: <span class="val">0.48</span>,
  <span class="kw">"filename"</span>: <span class="str">"api-design.md"</span>
}</div>
<p>The argument is <code>skill</code>, not <code>name</code>. The response has no <code>reason</code> field. <code>list_skills</code> also includes each recorded integrity value.</p>

<h2 id="trust">What a valid signature means</h2>
<p><code>VERIFIED</code> means consistency with this installation's current manifest and public key at the last rebuild. It does not establish who authored the content, whether sources are accurate, or whether instructions are safe.</p>
<p>The public key, private key, manifest, skills, and server process are all local. An attacker who controls the home directory or process can replace content and trust material together. The manifest itself is not anchored to an external key or transparency log.</p>

<h2 id="policy">A practical caller policy</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Restart or rebuild after expected external file changes.</li>
  <li>Call <code>skill_status</code> before loading content when integrity matters.</li>
  <li>Reject <code>TAMPERED</code>; decide explicitly whether <code>UNSIGNED</code> is allowed.</li>
  <li>Only then call <code>load_skill</code> or <code>load_section</code>.</li>
  <li>Still review <code>VERIFIED</code> content as potentially untrusted knowledge.</li>
</ol>

<p>For an actual enforced boundary, the server would need to verify at read time and refuse or attach status atomically. Version 3.1.0 does not yet do that.</p>
